I read the actual repo files and wrote you a deployment guide tailored to this repo and your Xubuntu laptop — not generic steps. Here's the short version of what to do:

The 5 steps:

Create a folder ~/soti-demo, put compose.yml and .env.template in it (that's all you need — images pull from Docker Hub).
cp .env.template .env and fill in the MobiControl block with your demo credentials — same Client ID / Secret / username / password your soti_daily_export.py already uses. Set TIMEZONE=Europe/Brussels. Pick an n8n password.
docker compose up -d then docker compose ps.
Open n8n at localhost:5678, build a Chat Trigger → AI Agent → MCP Client Tool flow.
Ask "give me a fleet health overview."
The four gotchas that will trip you up (all from the repo's own troubleshooting):

The MC container will show "(unhealthy)" — that's a false alarm. The healthcheck pings the wrong path. The curl in the guide proves it's actually fine.
In the n8n MCP tool, the endpoint is http://soti-mc-mcp:3000/mcp — the container name and internal port 3000, not localhost:4002. This is the #1 thing people get wrong.
Set the AI Agent's Max Tokens to 2048. n8n defaults to 65536 which causes 402 errors.
Give the agent the system prompt in the guide, or it'll keep asking "which project?" and claim there are no devices.
For your offline goal specifically: use your local Ollama as the chat model (Option A in the guide), not OpenRouter. One catch — you need to add extra_hosts: ["host.docker.internal:host-gateway"] to the n8n service so the container can reach Ollama on the host, and you must use a tool-calling capable model like qwen2.5:14b — the llama3.2:1b from your original install guide won't reliably call tools.

